# Playwright Demo Project

This project is a small example to learn how to write automated end-to-end tests with [Playwright](https://playwright.dev/).

## Prerequisites

- Node.js (recommended: LTS version)
- npm (comes with Node.js)
- Access to the internal application you want to test

## Setup

1. Create and open a new folder in your IDE or terminal.
2. Initialize Playwright:
   ```bash
   npm init playwright@latest
3.  Answer the questions in the wizard, e.g.
    Language: JavaScript
    Tests folder: tests
    Add GitHub Actions workflow: as you like


First Test
Create a file XXXYTest.spec.js in the tests folder:

// tests/XXXYTest.spec.js
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('First Playwright test', async ({ page }) => {
  // Step 1 – open the application
  await page.goto('https://your-internal-app.example');

  // Step 2 – enter username/password
  await page.fill('#username', 'my-username');
  await page.fill('#password', 'my-password');
  await page.click('button[type="submit"]');

  // Step 3 – simple verification
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);
});




# Running the Tests

1. Run all tests:
npx playwright test

2. Run tests in headed mode (see the browser):
npx playwright test --headed

# Important: async / await
Playwright commands are asynchronous.
Use await before each Playwright action to make sure the steps run in sequence:

await page.goto(...);
await page.fill(...);
await page.click(...);

3. Run only tagged tests, with tag @Web:
npx playwright test --grep @Web

test(` @Web ClientApp Buy And Checkout E2E for ${data.productName}`, async ({page})=>
   {

4. ${data.productName}` - will take the parameter from the JSON data source

5. See playwrigth.config1.js for different running configurations 
6. Test files run per default in parallel and the tests into one test file run in sequential, but when one test fails it goes on executing the next one.
7. When you add before the test : 
test.describe.configure({mode:'serial'}); //this should skipp the following tests, when a test fails
test.describe.configure({mode:'parallel'}); // this should run all tests from this test file in parallel.
8. Reporting:You can configure "reporter : 'html'" into the playwright.config.js and then copy the path of the html report from your project and paste it in the browser - so you open the last execution report.
9. Custom reporting Plugin - allure - install with the command: npm install -D allure-playwright  
   Then run test cases
   This is the command:
   npx playwright test --grep @Web --reporter=line,allure-playwright

   From the folder allure-results - generate the report:
   allure generate ./allure-results --clean

   Open the report: allure open

   If these commands are not working - allure not found, then install it in the commandline:
   npm install -D allure-commandline  
   and then run
   ./allure-results --clean
   npx allure open

10. In package.json you can save different scripts for running the tests. For example

"scripts": {
    "test": "npx playwright test tests/NetworkTest.spec.js --headed",
    "webTest": "npx playwright test --grep @Web",
    "apiTest": "npx playwright test --grep @API",
    "safariConfig1": "npx playwright test --config playwright.config1.js --project=safari"
  }, 


In the command promt you have to run this command for example:
npm run webTest